# `.claude/` is tracked: its README, an empty settings file and the change-discipline skill. What
# skein adds there in a box is not: the link to the store, the box's own settings, and an old
# untracked settings file skein moved aside (SKEIN-1053).
/.claude/skein
/.claude/settings.local.json
/.claude/settings.json.skein-old

# **Belt-and-braces since SKEIN-551, and it used to be the only defence.** `kit::ensure_store`
# scaffolded a store wherever it was pointed, an empty `"store"` in `repos.json` pointed it at
# nothing, and `create_dir_all("")` is `Ok(())` in Rust — so the layout landed in whatever directory
# the process was standing in, and a `git add -A` from the repo root once swept a whole scaffold
# into an unrelated commit (582d017 — `settings.json` and 28 files under `skein/`, every one a copy
# of `src/probe/` or `src/store/` or generated by `probes::ensure_probe_in`). `ensure_store` now
# refuses a store path that is not absolute and `registry::all_stores` yields none, so nothing
# should reach these names any more; the list stays because a denylist that has to grow with the
# layout is a bad first line of defence and a perfectly good second one. The canonical copies live
# in `src/store/` and `src/probe/`; nothing is meant to be at the root.
/skein/
/settings.json
# and the lock `util::update_json` takes beside it — same scaffold, same root, one file the
# block above predates because the settings write only started taking a lock in this change.
/.settings.json.lock
